WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
So, how do you build confidence as a

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.720
young person? So, a lot of people are

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.800
like under confident. They don't know

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.160
how to talk to strangers. So, what is

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:13.400
like your advice for them to, you know,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:16.280
become more confident and to start to

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.720
have these conversations with strangers

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.720
and how how can they go about doing

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.800
that?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.120
I think it's a it's so important and I'm

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.080
so glad you asked and I actually feel

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.640
very passionately about parenting now

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.680
because I have young children that I'm

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.160
trying to instill these things in

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.599
because it starts at a very young age.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.160
You went to college at a at a high-end

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.520
college in in the United States, so did

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.080
I. I'm sure you saw a lot of people all

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.960
the way around you who

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.680
their parents did everything for them.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.880
Like their parents put them in a bubble.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.920
Their parents made decisions. Their

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.320
parents bought their books. Their

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.440
parents scheduled their Ubers. Their

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.480
parents picked up and picked their

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.480
classes. All of it. Um

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.520
you have to practice making decisions

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.880
and living with the consequences to get

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.880
that confidence.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.000
And you have to practice talking to

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.720
people and be forced to talk to people

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.840
at a very young age. So, I like I like

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.880
doing a lot of things with my kids that

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.920
kind of get them a little bit out of

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.240
outside of their comfort zone when it

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.720
comes to talking to adults, socializing

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.960
with adults. But if you're in college or

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.400
out of college and you still feel like

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.960
you're an introvert,

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.680
you have to just understand that it's

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.080
you can change you can change. It's it's

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.440
trained. It's a muscle. Your ability to

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.480
be comfortable in uncomfortable

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.080
situations is a muscle.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.360
And the only way to build muscle is to

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.040
go to the gym and lift the weight. You

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.080
can't read about muscle. You can't study

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.960
muscle. You can't just build workout

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.680
plans and eat protein. You have to go to

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.520
the gym and lift weights.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.640
So, that means you have to get out and

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.800
practice and you have to sign up for a

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.720
debate club or go to public speaking

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.440
class or do some of these things to

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.760
build the muscle so that you start to

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.840
feel comfortable being uncomfortable. It

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.720
never gets easy.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.280
You know, walking into a podcast

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.560
interview every single time before I hit

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.840
enter room,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.400
it's always a little bit uncomfortable,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.320
always a little bit of nerves, but you

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.440
just get used to um handling it. So,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.480
that's the advice I have for sure.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.120
Amazing, amazing. And I just would love

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.400
to end you with your hot takes on

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.280
college and university. And what would

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.240
you have done differently now that you

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.320
you know, you've gone through the

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.080
university system of the US and you have

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.360
some of your own kind of takes on it as

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.280
well. I can see on X some some things

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.560
that you posted. So, we just love to

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.680
like distill like what are some words

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.520
that you have on college and

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.840
universities?

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.240
So, first of all, I I think

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.000
college is still very important. I'm

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.480
still going to send my kids to college.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.400
Um a lot of people say college is

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.880
worthless. I don't think that's true. I

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.680
I as an 18-year-old I was not ready to

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.360
go get married and get a job and start

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.000
my life. So, I spent college learning

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.280
how to socialize, learning how to you

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.000
know, growing up, learning how to sell

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.720
myself and my ideas,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.000
um learning how to get in relationships

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.280
with people, um and date and do all the

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.840
important things in life that are not

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.840
necessarily in the text in the in the

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.400
room in the classroom.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.560
Um but

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.920
I'm kind of down on super high-end

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.800
colleges at the moment because I think

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.920
they're a ton of work to get into. They

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.880
don't necessarily reward a balanced life

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.120
in high school. And so yeah, I I think

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.440
I'm going to send my kids to

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.400
a southern state school that's not

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.640
necessarily a top academic university,

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.960
but has a lot of normal kids with normal

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.680
values and

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.680
and uh you know, go from there.

